A start may run past the memory end's frame.
The controller would read or write memory its holder does not hold, the kernel's among it;
a channel reaches only memory within its memory end's frame.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: dma's channel reaches memory beyond its memory end
Witness: fuzz dma-starts-within-its-frame

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -970,9 +970,6 @@ static int op_dma(struct thread *t, const struct cap *cap, uint32_t op, const ui
         }
         /* The range lies in the memory end's frame, which holds it as long as the transfer may reach it. */
         const struct cap *memory = &d->ends[dmac_memory(d->channel)];
-        if (offset > block_size(memory) || length > block_size(memory) - offset) {
-            return KERR_INVALID_ARG;
-        }
         uint32_t device = dmac_memory(d->channel) == DMA_SOURCE ? DMA_SINK : DMA_SOURCE;
         dmac_start(d->channel, dma_word(d, device), block_base(memory) + offset, length);
         return KERR_OK;
